WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.340
Welcome back!

2
00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:06.619
Yesterday we received the school's alerts and checked them for red flags.

3
00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.138
Today we assess them.

4
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:15.226
Assessing means asking the same five questions, in the same order, for every alert: is it routine,

5
00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:22.603
what really happened, how dangerous is it, how important is the computer, and therefore what priority,

6
00:00:22.603 --> 00:00:24.484
and does it need a ticket.

7
00:00:24.484 --> 00:00:27.087
Let's do it for the six demo alerts.

8
00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:31.972
This is the priority matrix from SOC-SOP-02.

9
00:00:31.972 --> 00:00:36.960
The threat level is the row, the asset tier is the column.

10
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.971
A critical threat is P1 everywhere.

11
00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:46.164
A high threat is P1 on a Tier 1 computer but P2 on a Tier 2 workstation.

12
00:00:47.263 --> 00:00:49.229
Now what really happened.

13
00:00:49.229 --> 00:00:53.002
The rule name only says what the tool suspected.

14
00:00:53.002 --> 00:00:55.360
The details say what happened.

15
00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.255
Format-List shows every alert in full, so the long details are not cut off.

16
00:01:02.355 --> 00:01:07.199
D5-06 is a Wazuh agent keep-alive: routine, rule RT6.

17
00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:09.209
Record it and move on.

18
00:01:09.209 --> 00:01:23.007
D5-01 looked like a brute force, but the details say the registrar typed her password at her own desk with Caps Lock on, and she confirmed it by phone.

19
00:01:23.007 --> 00:01:27.119
Nothing malicious happened: a false positive.

20
00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.221
No priority, no ticket.

21
00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:36.531
D5-03, inbound SSH from the internet, was denied: a blocked attempt, low threat.

22
00:01:37.632 --> 00:01:40.039
Three alerts are confirmed.

23
00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:49.314
D5-02 and D5-05 are red flags, ransomware and a file-infecting virus: always confirmed, always critical.

24
00:01:49.314 --> 00:01:57.786
And D5-04: the student grades file really left the school, because the DLP policy only watches.

25
00:01:57.786 --> 00:02:03.048
Sensitive data that left the organization is a high threat.

26
00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:09.273
Last criterion: how important is each computer?

27
00:02:09.273 --> 00:02:12.545
The asset list gives the tier.

28
00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:17.889
REG-PC-01 holds student records, so it is Tier 1.

29
00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:21.706
LIB-PC-02 and MKT-PC-04 are Tier 2.

30
00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:25.195
The internet firewall is Tier 1.

31
00:02:26.295 --> 00:02:28.886
Here is the finished assessment.

32
00:02:28.886 --> 00:02:35.606
Notice D5-04: a high threat on a Tier 1 computer is P1, the same as the ransomware.

33
00:02:35.606 --> 00:02:44.593
And D5-03 is only P3 even though the firewall is Tier 1, because the attempt was blocked and the threat is low.

34
00:02:44.593 --> 00:02:51.231
Three confirmed detections are three different incidents, so I open three tickets.

35
00:02:52.331 --> 00:02:53.165
Your turn.

36
00:02:53.165 --> 00:02:57.669
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 6 and Activity 6.1.

37
00:02:57.669 --> 00:03:05.674
Assess all thirteen alerts from yesterday's queue: detection, threat, tier, priority and ticket.

38
00:03:05.674 --> 00:03:10.427
Careful: alerts about the same incident share one ticket.

